  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-2aad048247f190ef": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-679",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The “tormentors” are the jailers, who were allowed to scourge and torture the poor debtors in their care in order to get money from them for the grasping creditors, or else to excite the compassion of friends, and obtain the amount of the debt from them. “In early times of Rome t…"
      },
      "c-4629a46df6b0c191": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-677",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 15:4,7. This mode of expression was very common with the Jews. Lightfoot cites from the Talmud a passage illustrative of it: “When a man is dividing nuts among the poor, though ninety-nine call upon him to divide them, and one call on him to scatter them, to him the…"
      },
      "c-7362f9823178444b": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-678",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This is a common Oriental form of speech to express an indefinite number. There are other instances of it in the Bible. See 1 Kings 17:12; Isaiah 7:21; 17:6; Jeremiah 3:14; Hosea 6:2. Some commentators, however, suppose the passage in Hosea to refer to the resurrection of Christ;…"
      },
      "c-c5940287df0a5d02": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-676",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 9:42; Luke 17:2."
      }
    },
